reading('an-imperfect-apology','An Imperfect Apology','C1','Relationships',
"""At a team dinner, Omar made a joke about a colleague’s presentation that he intended to sound lighthearted. The comment embarrassed Nina, who had worked all week on the project. The next morning, Omar sent a message saying he was sorry if she had been offended. Nina replied that the problem was not her sensitivity but his public remark. Omar realized that his wording had avoided taking responsibility. He asked to speak privately, acknowledged that the joke had been inappropriate, and apologized without adding an excuse. Nina appreciated the second conversation, although she said trust would take time to rebuild. During the next team meeting, Omar credited Nina’s contribution clearly. They did not become close friends immediately, but their working relationship gradually improved because his behavior became more considerate.""",[
('What initially upset Nina?','A public joke about her presentation',['A missed dinner invitation','A canceled business trip','A correction to her email'],'Omar joked about her work in front of colleagues.'),
('Why was Omar’s first apology inadequate?','It made her reaction the issue instead of his behavior',['It was delivered too late at night','It was handwritten','It mentioned a different project'],'“Sorry if you were offended” avoided accepting responsibility.'),
('How did Omar change his apology?','He acknowledged his inappropriate remark without excuses',['He blamed Nina again','He criticized the team','He refused to meet privately'],'He took responsibility and apologized directly.'),
('What did Nina say about rebuilding trust?','It would take time',['One message had fixed everything','It depended only on the manager','The issue could never improve'],'Nina appreciated the discussion but cautioned that trust needed time.'),
('What later action supported Omar’s apology?','Giving Nina clear credit in a meeting',['Avoiding all communication','Repeating the joke','Taking credit for her work'],'Omar publicly recognized Nina’s contribution in a later meeting.')])
reading('the-longer-route','The Longer Route','B2','Travel & Cities',
"""Dylan usually drove across the city on a busy highway, but road construction forced him to take a longer route through residential streets. He initially complained because the new trip added ten minutes. After a week, he noticed that the neighborhood route was more predictable: traffic moved slowly but rarely stopped completely. He also discovered a small bakery where he could pick up breakfast before work. When the highway reopened, Dylan compared travel times rather than automatically returning to his old habit. The highway was faster on some mornings but much slower after minor accidents. Dylan began checking conditions before leaving and chose the quieter route when delays were likely. He realized that the shortest distance did not always produce the most reliable journey.""",[
('Why did Dylan change his usual journey?','Highway road construction',['His car had broken down','He moved to a different house','He started cycling to work'],'Construction forced him off his usual highway.'),
('How much longer did the alternate drive first seem?','Ten minutes',['Five minutes','Half an hour','An entire hour'],'The route initially added about ten minutes.'),
('What advantage did he notice after a week?','More predictable traffic',['Free parking on every street','No traffic lights','A much shorter distance'],'Traffic was slow but more consistent.'),
('What did he discover along the route?','A small bakery',['A new railway station','An airport shuttle','A public library'],'He found a bakery for breakfast.'),
('How did his commuting habit change?','He checked conditions and selected the more reliable route',['He permanently avoided every highway','He drove without considering traffic','He traveled only on weekends'],'Dylan chose routes based on actual conditions, not habit.')])
reading('the-concert-ticket','A Ticket Worth Keeping','B2','Social Life',
"""Rina bought two concert tickets months in advance because her brother loved the band. A week before the show, he learned that his final exam had been moved to the following morning. He decided not to attend, even though he was disappointed. Rina offered the spare ticket to a friend, but the friend could only arrive halfway through the performance. Instead of letting the seat remain empty, Rina posted a message in her neighborhood group offering it at face value. A teacher who had recently moved to the city replied and bought the ticket. They met outside the venue, enjoyed the show, and discovered they lived on the same street. Rina sent her brother a short video afterward. He appreciated the gesture and said he would plan another concert after exams.""",[
('Why could Rina’s brother not attend?','His final exam was moved to the next morning',['He had lost his ticket','The band canceled','He had moved abroad'],'A rescheduled exam conflicted with the concert.'),
('Why was Rina’s friend not an ideal replacement?','The friend could arrive only halfway through',['The friend disliked all music','The friend had already bought a ticket','The friend was performing with the band'],'The friend could not attend the full show.'),
('How did Rina offer the spare ticket?','In a neighborhood group at face value',['Through an expensive auction','By leaving it outside the venue','By mailing it to the band'],'Rina offered the spare seat locally without a markup.'),
('Who bought the ticket?','A teacher new to the city',['The venue manager','The original ticket seller','Her brother’s examiner'],'A recently arrived teacher responded.'),
('What did Rina and the buyer discover?','They lived on the same street',['They had attended the same school','They were relatives','They had worked together previously'],'They found out they were neighbors.')])
reading('the-faulty-metric','The Wrong Metric','C1','Business & Analytics',
"""A retail chain celebrated after its new loyalty program attracted thousands of sign-ups. Senior managers pointed to the growing registration chart as proof of success. Analyst Imani was less convinced. She discovered that many customers created accounts for a one-time discount but never returned. She proposed examining repeat purchases and customer complaints alongside sign-ups. The deeper analysis revealed that customers who joined in person returned more often than those recruited through online advertisements. Rather than cancel the campaign, Imani recommended testing a follow-up welcome message and improving the checkout experience. Over the next quarter, the proportion of returning customers rose slightly. The team learned to distinguish a metric that looked impressive from one that actually reflected lasting customer relationships.""",[
('Which measure originally impressed management?','Number of loyalty-program sign-ups',['Profit per store','Average delivery distance','Employee attendance'],'Managers focused on the growing registration count.'),
('Why did Imani question the interpretation?','Many members never made a second purchase',['The program had no members','Every customer complained immediately','Stores had stopped selling products'],'Some people joined for a discount and did not return.'),
('What did she suggest measuring instead or as well?','Repeat purchases and customer complaints',['Only the number of advertisements','The company logo size','How many employees wore uniforms'],'She proposed metrics tied to customer behavior and experience.'),
('Which enrollment channel brought more returning shoppers?','In-person enrollment',['Online advertisements','Printed newspaper ads','Radio competitions'],'The data indicated higher repeat visits for in-person sign-ups.'),
('What lesson did the team learn?','An impressive-looking figure may not reflect lasting value',['Large numbers always mean success','Customer feedback is useless','Every online campaign should be canceled'],'The team separated vanity metrics from real customer relationships.')])
G=[
('used-to-and-be-used-to','Used to vs be used to','B2','I used to commute by train, but now I am used to driving.','Used to + base verb describes a past habit that no longer applies. Be used to + noun or -ing means something feels familiar now.','After six months, she is used to ___ early.','getting up',['get up','got up','getted up']),
('should-have-vs-must-have','Should have vs must have','B2','You should have called earlier. / She must have missed the train.','Should have + past participle expresses criticism or regret about an earlier choice. Must have + past participle is a strong deduction about a past event.','He is not answering. He ___ fallen asleep.','must have',['should have','must','would']),
('would-rather-and-prefer','Would rather vs would prefer','B2','I would rather stay home, but I would prefer to leave early tomorrow.','Would rather + bare infinitive; would prefer + to-infinitive. Both express preferences, with different structures.','We would rather ___ before rush hour.','leave',['to leave','leaving','left']),
('despite-in-spite-of-although','Despite vs although','B2','Despite the rain, we went out. / Although it was raining, we went out.','Despite / in spite of is followed by a noun or -ing clause. Although is followed by a subject and finite verb.','___ being exhausted, Maya finished the report.','Despite',['Although','Because','However']),
('reported-questions','Reported questions: word order','B2','She asked me where I lived. / He asked if I needed help.','Reported questions use statement word order; yes/no questions usually take if or whether. Do not preserve the original question inversion.','He asked me where I ___ .','worked',['did work','do work','working']),
('future-perfect','Future perfect for deadlines','B2','By Friday, we will have finished the project.','Use will have + past participle for an action expected to be complete before a specific future time.','By the time you arrive, I ___ dinner.','will have cooked',['will cook','have cooked','had cooked']),
('the-causative-have-get','Causative have / get something done','B2','I had my phone repaired yesterday.','Have/get + object + past participle describes arranging for another person to perform a service. The subject is not necessarily the person doing the work.','We are getting the roof ___ next week.','repaired',['repair','repairing','to repair']),
('inversion-hardly-when','Hardly ... when: narrative inversion','C1','Hardly had we sat down when the fire alarm rang.','When hardly appears at the start with a past-perfect sequence, invert the auxiliary and subject: Hardly had + subject + past participle + when.','Hardly ___ the presentation when the lights went out.','had she begun',['she had begun','has she begun','did she began']),
('not-only-but-also-inversion','Not only ... but also with inversion','C1','Not only did the hotel lose our booking, but it also charged us twice.','When not only introduces a clause at the beginning, invert the auxiliary and subject in the first clause. Do not invert the second clause.','Not only ___ the deadline, but he also forgot the attachment.','did he miss',['he missed','had he miss','he did missed']),
('wish-past-perfect-regret','Wish + past perfect for regret','C1','I wish I had checked the address before leaving.','Wish + had + past participle expresses regret about something that happened or failed to happen in the past. Wish + past simple instead expresses a present unreal situation.','I wish we ___ the earlier flight yesterday.','had taken',['take','would take','have taking'])]

def normalized(entry):
    id,title,level,example,explanation,question,correct,wrong=entry
    opts=[correct]+wrong
    assert len(opts)==4 and len(set(opts))==4 and len(explanation)>90
    idx=sum(map(ord,id))%4
    opts=opts[-idx:]+opts[:-idx] if idx else opts
    assert opts.index(correct)==idx
    return dict(id=id,title=title,level=level,example=example,explanation=explanation,question=question,answer=correct,answerIndex=idx,options=opts)

def process():
    read_path=ROOT/'data/readings.json'; gram_path=ROOT/'data/grammar.json'
    readings=json.loads(read_path.read_text()); gram=json.loads(gram_path.read_text())
    existing_r={x['id'] for x in readings};existing_g={x['id'] for x in gram}
    new_readings=0
    for x in R:
        if x['id'] in existing_r:
            continue
        new_readings+=1
        assert len(x['body'].split())>=90, (x['id'],len(x['body'].split()))
        questions=[]
        for i,(q,right,wrong,evidence) in enumerate(x['questions']):
            assert len(wrong)==3 and len(set([right]+wrong))==4
            assert len(evidence)>=25,(x['id'],q,evidence)
            opts=[right]+wrong
            shift=(i+sum(map(ord,x['id'])))%4
            opts=opts[shift:]+opts[:shift]
            questions.append(dict(question=q,options=opts,answer=opts.index(right),explanation=evidence))
        readings.append(dict(id=x['id'],title=x['title'],level=x['level'],category=x['category'],body=x['body'],questions=questions))
    new_grammar=0
    for x in G:
        n=normalized(x)
        if n['id'] in existing_g:
            continue
        new_grammar+=1
        gram.append(n)
    assert len(readings)==30 and len(gram)==20,(len(readings),len(gram))
    read_path.write_text(json.dumps(readings,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    gram_path.write_text(json.dumps(gram,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    report={'readings':len(readings),'newReadingsThisRun':new_readings,'grammar':len(gram),'newGrammarThisRun':new_grammar,
            'newReadingQuestionsAuthored':sum(len(x['questions']) for x in R),'source':'Original in-project editorial content, not a borrowed transcript',
            'validation':'Structural checks only; no independent language-editor approval'}
    (ROOT/'qa/reading-grammar-expansion.json').write_text(json.dumps(report,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    print(json.dumps(report,ensure_ascii=False))
if __name__=='__main__':process()
